<h2>Why sunset times matter for your timer</h2>
<p class="lead">In Spring, Texas, sunset swings by roughly three hours across the year, from around 5:20 p.m. in early December to after 8:20 p.m. in late June. A fixed-time timer set in summer leaves your home dark for hours in winter.</p>
<h2>Timer options compared</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>How it works</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Mechanical timer</td><td>Fixed on/off times</td><td>Needs adjusting through the seasons and after power outages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Photocell</td><td>Turns on when it gets dark</td><td>Runs dusk to dawn unless paired with a timer; can be fooled by shade or nearby lights</td></tr>
<tr><td>Astronomical timer</td><td>Calculates sunset for your location every day</td><td>Must be set to the right location and time zone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smart / app control</td><td>Astronomical scheduling plus zones, dimming and remote control</td><td>Needs reliable Wi-Fi or a hub near the transformer</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Recommended schedules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Architectural and tree lighting:</strong> about 15 minutes after sunset until 11 p.m. or midnight.</li>
  <li><strong>Path and entry lighting:</strong> sunset until midnight, or later on a separate zone if you come home late.</li>
  <li><strong>Patio lighting:</strong> on demand, or a short evening window.</li>
</ul>
<p>Turning decorative layers off at a reasonable hour cuts energy use and reduces light spilling toward neighbors. Plug the yearly run-hours above into the <a href="/tools/energy-cost-calculator/">energy cost calculator</a> to see what your schedule costs, or let Spring Landscape Lighting set up <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart controls</a> with zones that follow the sun automatically.</p>
